use "testing" ;

\ Combinator-entry contention benchmark for the frame-lock removal.
\
\ This spawns many tasks that each churn combinator frames concurrently. Under
\ the shared write lock, every combinator frame push / pop across all workers
\ serializes on one global lock; after 349.4 the transient push / pop takes no
\ lock.
\
\ Run under `--threads=auto --compile=off` and compare the benchmark `user_ns`
\ before vs after the lock removal.

\\ `call` and `if` each push and pop a transient local frame. `times` drives the
\\ inner loop, itself a combinator. Each task issues a long, tight run of frame
\\ operations; running roughly two such tasks per worker keeps every worker
\\ hammering the frame-entry path in parallel, which is what contends on the
\\ shared lock in the pre-349.4 build. The task count is kept low so scheduler /
\\ spawn overhead does not swamp the frame-lock signal.
churn: ( -- ) [
  1500 [
    5 [ 1 + ] call drop
    2 3 < [ 10 ] [ 20 ] if drop
    7 [ 2 * ] call drop
  ] times
] ;

[
  28 [
    [ churn ] spawn drop
  ] times
] task-scope

t "combinator contention benchmark completed" assert-true
